":"retired","retired_at":"2026-10-27T08:58:47.943104+00:00","reason_code":"duplicate_diagnostic_target"},{"id":"year_1_2a_math_iii_analysis_big_o_and_little_o_notations_c490e3b9_004","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"application","question":"As $x\\to 0$, what is stated about $\\sin(x^2)$ relative to $x^2$?","options":["$\\sin(x^2)$ is $o(x^2)$.","$\\sin(x^2)$ is not $O(x^2)$.","$x^2$ is not comparable to $\\sin(x^2)$ in any way.","$\\sin(x^2)$ is $O(x^2)$ but not $o(x^2)$."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"b6efc9601db062e89e18342f","time_limit":30,"curation_status":"retired","retired_at":"2026-10-27T08:58:47.943104+00:00","reason_code":"duplicate_diagnostic_target"},{"id":"year_1_2a_math_iii_analysis_big_o_and_little_o_notations_c490e3b9_005","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"conceptual","question":"Which notation makes a stronger assertion?","options":["Big-$O$","Little-$o$","They are always equivalent","Neither notation compares functions"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"5d50a5f49b9875a106ce52e0","time_limit":30,"curation_status":"retired","retired_at":"2026-10-27T08:58:47.943104+00:00","reason_code":"duplicate_diagnostic_target"},{"id":"year_1_2a_math_iii_analysis_big_o_and_little_o_notations_c490e3b9_008","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"application","question":"As $x\\to 0$, what is true for $f(x) = x^3$ and $g(x) = x^2$?","options":["$g(x) = o(f(x))$","$f(x)$ is not $O(g(x))$","$f(x)/g(x)$ tends to $1$","$f(x) = o(g(x))$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"ebcacc43cb24f525348489af","time_limit":30,"curation_status":"retired","retired_at":"2026-10-27T08:58:47.943104+00:00","reason_code":"duplicate_diagnostic_target"},{"id":"year_1_2a_math_iii_analysis_big_o_and_little_o_notations_c490e3b9_010","difficulty":"challenge","question_type":"conceptual","question":"Why does $f(x) = O(g(x))$ not by itself imply $f(x) = o(g(x))$?","options":["Big-$O$ requires the quotient to tend to $0$.","Big-$O$ only gives a bound, while little-$o$ requires the quotient to tend to $0$.","Little-$o$ is weaker than big-$O$.","The two notations mean the same thing, so a bound already forces the quotient to tend to $0$."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"0bf0f3b61a52bab144f9f9c2","time_limit":40,"curation_status":"retired","retired_at":"2026-10-27T08:58:47.943104+00:00","reason_code":"duplicate_diagnostic_target"},{"id":"year_1_2a_math_iii_analysis_big_o_and_little_o_notations_c490e3b9_011","difficulty":"challenge","question_type":"application","question":"As $x\\to 0$, is $x = O(x^2)$?","options":["Yes, because $x/x^2\\to 0$.","No, because $|x|/x^2 = 1/|x|$ is not bounded near $0$.","Yes, because every real function is $O(x^2)$ near the point $0$.","No, because $x^2$ is strictly negative near $0$."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"e2c93c7fce8ecc1daef213f4","time_limit":40,"curation_status":"retired","retired_at":"2026-10-27T08:58:47.943104+00:00","reason_code":"duplicate_diagnostic_target"},{"id":"expansion_big_o_and_little_o_notations_013","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"conceptual","question":"What kind of bound defines $f(x)=O(g(x))$ as $x\\to a$?","options":["$|f(x)|\\le Mg(x)$ near $a$ for some $M \\gt 0$","$f(x)/g(x)\\to\\infty$","$f(x)=g(x)$ for all $x$","$g(x)=0$ in every punctured neighbourhood"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"ca761a21edc7a1e85865ea68","time_limit":25,"curation_status":"retired","retired_at":"2026-10-27T08:58:47.943104+00:00","reason_code":"replaced_after_academic_review"},{"id":"expansion_big_o_and_little_o_notations_015","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"conceptual","question":"Let $a\\in\\mathbb{R}$ and $g(x)\\neq0$ near $a$. Which statement implies $f(x)=O(g(x))$ as $x\\to a$, but is not implied by it?","options":["$g(x)=O(f(x))$ as $x\\to a$","$|f(x)|\\ge c\\,|g(x)|$ near $a$ for some $c>0$","$f(x)=o(g(x))$ as $x\\to a$","$f(a)=g(a)$ at the point $a$ itself"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"336f01251bcb5d03304c9bf6","time_limit":35,"curation_status":"retired","retired_at":"2026-10-27T08:58:47.943104+00:00","reason_code":"replaced_after_academic_review"},{"id":"expansion_big_o_and_little_o_notations_016","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"application","question":"As $x\\to0$, which statement is true for $x^3$ relative to $x^2$?","options":["$x^3=o(x^2)$","$x^2=o(x^3)$","$x^3$ is not $O(x^2)$","$x^3/x^2\\to1$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"b04bba7c1dfc5912c928ec8b","time_limit":25,"curation_status":"retired","retired_at":"2026-10-27T08:58:47.943104+00:00","reason_code":"duplicate_diagnostic_target"},{"id":"expansion_big_o_and_little_o_notations_017","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"application","question":"As $x\\to0$, which statement is true for $x^2$ relative to $x^2$?","options":["$x^2=o(x^2)$, hence also $x^2=O(x^2)$","$x^2=O(x^2)$ but $x^2\\ne o(x^2)$","$x^2\\ne O(x^2)$, since $x^2\\to0$","Neither, as $x^2/x^2$ is undefined at $0$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"2a7c5caf1143ffb4be7b8429","time_limit":30,"curation_status":"retired","retired_at":"2026-10-27T08:58:47.943104+00:00","reason_code":"duplicate_diagnostic_target"},{"id":"expansion_big_o_and_little_o_notations_018","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"application","question":"As $x\\to0$, why is $5x^3=o(x)$?","options":["Because $5x^3/x\\to5$","Because $x$ is never positive","Because $5x^3/x=5x^2\\to0$","Because little-$o$ ignores ratios"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"a50965f20c335c33155b3ea9","time_limit":20,"curation_status":"retired","retired_at":"2026-10-27T08:58:47.943104+00:00","reason_code":"duplicate_diagnostic_target"},{"id":"expansion_big_o_and_little_o_notations_019","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"conceptual","question":"If $f(x)\\sim g(x)$ as $x\\to a$, which big-$O$ relations follow?","options":["Only $f(x)=o(g(x))$","Only $g(x)=o(f(x))$","Neither function can be bounded by the other","$f(x)=O(g(x))$ and $g(x)=O(f(x))$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"9f10bc0feee3ee5d38bd2b42","time_limit":30,"curation_status":"retired","retired_at":"2026-10-27T08:58:47.943104+00:00","reason_code":"duplicate_diagnostic_target"}]}}